Hello Gustavo, welcome to the group.

Let me know if I have misunderstood any of this, but here is how I understand your survey design.

Six vegetation types (you are referring to these as "sites") and you wish to have an estimate of bird abundance or density for each vegetation type.

If this is accurate, I would create a table or spreadsheet of data in this manner:

Study area - one name (Patagonia)
Region - six vegetation types
Point transect - name of point
Observation (distance) - midpoint of bin in which detection was made (0-10m=5, 10-20m=15, 20-30m=25,...)

There is a sample project that ships with Distance for Windows (in the folder Sample Projects) that resembles your study. It is called "House Wren" and is a study of Troglodytes aedon in the western U.S.  The data in Distance for Windows looks like this:

"Study area" would be as I describe above, you would not need the "Visits" column as you will provide that in the "Survey effort" column later. Your first region label could be "Ñire open Forest" and the associate size of this vegetation type. Your points could be labelled anything that makes sense to you, and Survey effort would be the number of visits to the point (3 I think you said). Finally the Radial distance would be the midpoint of your distance bands. You would not need the final "Observer" column as in the house wren data.

Note, the number of transects is three or four as you noted, not three or four multiplied by the number of visits (which is how I suspect you arrived at the number 12 in your note).

I hope this is helpful.

